{% note
   text="SUID and SGID are special Linux permission bits.\n\nSUID allows an executable to run with the permissions of its owner, while SGID allows it to run with the permissions of its group.\n\nWhen these permissions are applied to unusual or vulnerable binaries, they can become an interesting privilege-escalation path." /%}

### Finding SUID & SGID Files

{% terminal
   title="permission enumeration"
   commands=[{cmd: "find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null"}, {cmd: "find / -perm -2000 -type f 2>/dev/null"}, {cmd: "ls -la /usr/bin/passwd"}] /%}

{% twoColumn
   left="SUID is represented by:\n\ns in the owner's execute position.\n\nExample:\n\n-rwsr-xr-x\n\nThe executable runs with the owner's privileges."
   right="SGID is represented by:\n\ns in the group's execute position.\n\nExample:\n\n-rwxr-sr-x\n\nThe executable runs with the group's privileges." /%}

{% finding
   severity="informational"
   text="Pay attention to:\n\n• SUID binaries in unusual locations\n• Custom binaries with SUID/SGID\n• Unexpected ownership\n• Binaries writable by unprivileged users\n• Recently created privileged executables\n• Applications with known privilege-escalation paths" /%}

{% warning
   text="Many legitimate Linux utilities use SUID.\n\nFinding a SUID binary is only an enumeration result. Check what the binary does, who owns it, its permissions, and whether it provides an exploitable execution path." /%}

{% tip
   text="Standard system binaries are usually less interesting than unexpected SUID/SGID executables found in custom directories." /%}

### Takeaway

Always enumerate SUID and SGID files during Linux privilege-escalation assessments, but treat the results as leads that require further investigation.
